Serial triangular solve and a CHECKED multi-threaded factorization in SPOOLES (GPL-2.0, like CalculiX itself; a change of the solver logic of
CalculiX 2.23 made by FEA Studio). Applied by installer/windows/build_ccx.sh and installer/linux/build_ccx.sh with `patch -p1` in the
folder that contains CalculiX/ccx_2.23/. The same file lies in installer/windows/patches/ and installer/linux/patches/.
Dates of the change: 20. 11. 2026 (first version), 21. 11. 2026 (review: row-wise check, verdict of the serial repeat, bookkeeping error of the threads,
link proof of the ordered SPOOLES), 23. 11. 2026 (fea_judge: a violation of the bookkeeping is judged before the off-switches; a repeat whose test solve could
not be done is not called confirmed) and 24. 11. 2026 (review of the integrated 1.5.1 line: after the verdict "the matrix" the later factorizations of the process run
on ONE thread; the marker is FEA_SPOOLES_FACTOR_CHECK=2, the value of the first fea_judge was 1). GPL-2.0 section 2a: the modified files carry a notice that they were changed and when; the comments "FEA Studio change,
21. 11. 2026", "23. 11. 2026" and "24. 11. 2026" of the hunks below are that notice in spooles.c and ccx_2.23.c, the printf of the first hunk is the notice at the program start.
The text above the first hunk header is ignored by patch(1); only the hunks (from the first minus-minus-minus line on) are the same for the Windows and the Linux build.

Why 1 (serial solve): with CCX_NPROC_EQUATION_SOLVER > 1 ccx 2.23 factorizes with FrontMtx_MT_factorInpMtx and solves with FrontMtx_MT_solve (via
fsolve_MT in spooles.c). The multi-threaded solve is a data race: eigenvalue and buckling decks, which solve many right hand sides with one factorization,
returned wrong and run-to-run different results without any message from 3 threads on (buckling B1: factor 0.99 instead of 21.65 with 4 threads; the
dhondt.de build behaves the same). The change: fsolve_MT calls the serial FrontMtx_solve. fsolve_MT is the only caller of FrontMtx_MT_solve in CalculiX,
and spooles_solve and spooles_solve_rad (every static, eigenvalue / ARPACK, buckling, dynamic and radiation solve) reach it only there, so this one call
covers every place where the multi-threaded solve was used.

Why 2 (checked factorization): the multi-threaded FACTORIZATION of SPOOLES (FrontMtx_MT_factorInpMtx) is not safe either. In rare runs (3 in 1 260 modal /
buckling analyses with 4 threads on a 16 core machine; not reproduced in 2 600 runs pinned to two cores and in 8 000 factorizations with random delays at the
hand-over points; ThreadSanitizer reports only the intended lock-free hand-over) the aggregate chevron of one thread for one front is silently not assembled
by the thread that owns the front: the factors are those of another matrix, and the eigenvalues of the saved wrong run (0.7234915E+07 0.8180524E+07
0.5683356E+09 0.5164242E+10 instead of 0.6534415E+06 0.6534491E+06 0.2605042E+09 0.5024819E+09) are reproduced to the last digit by dropping exactly that
chevron. The mechanism of the loss was not found. The change makes the result safe without knowing it: spooles_factor and spooles_factor_rad solve one pseudo
random right hand side (|b_i| between 0.25 and 1) with the new factors and compute the ROW-WISE backward error
max_i |b_i - (A x)_i| / ( sum_j |a_ij| |x_j| + |b_i| ) with the matrix that they handed to SPOOLES (all five input formats): about 1e-16 for a correct
factorization, and every row is judged by its own size, so a lost update is as visible in a soft part of a model with a stiffness ratio of 1e5 as in a stiff one
(the first version used a norm of the whole matrix and a limit of 1e-9, which scales with the stiffest row). Above 1e-12 (environment variable
FEA_SPOOLES_CHECK_TOL changes the limit, 0 switches the check off for tests; the program removes the variable from the environment of ccx unless FEA_DEV=1) the
factors are dropped and the matrix is factorized again with the same threads and, if that fails too, with ONE thread (no hand-over between threads). The
factorization on one thread is checked too: when it passes, the loss in the threads is confirmed ("threaded loss confirmed"); when it fails as well, the cause is
the matrix ("the matrix, not the threads"), the factors are used as they are and the check is switched off for the rest of the process (a static flag), so a
matrix that no factorization can pass does not make every later factorization run three times. The threaded factorization is safe only while it is checked, so
(24. 11. 2026) every LATER factorization of that process runs on ONE thread: `(num_cpus > 1)&&(fea_attempt < 2)&&(!fea_check_off)` in spooles_factor and
spooles_factor_rad (before this change the later systems ran unchecked on the threads). SPOOLES itself reports a violation of its own bookkeeping of the
hand-over (see spooles_2.2_ordered_assembly.patch: every list empty, every count zero, chevrons sent = taken = assembled at the end of the factorization) with
the error code -2, which is treated like a failed check; that verdict is made BEFORE the off-switches (the flag of an earlier matrix that no factorization passes, and
the test limit 0), because it needs no test solve. A check that cannot be done (no memory for the test solve) is reported with a warning, and a repeated
factorization whose test solve could not be done is used "without a verdict": it is never called "confirmed". The cost is one solve and one matrix-vector product.

The first hunk makes `ccx -v` print the lines FEA_SPOOLES_SERIAL_SOLVE=1, FEA_SPOOLES_FACTOR_CHECK=2 and FEA_SPOOLES_ORDERED_ASSEMBLY=1 (only in a build with
USE_MT): markers that the program reads from the executable (fea_core.ccx.runner.spooles_threads_safe) before it allows more than one equation-solver thread.
The third line is printed only when fea_spooles_ordered() (spooles.c) is called, and that function takes the address of FrontMtx_updateOrdered, which exists only in
the SPOOLES of spooles_2.2_ordered_assembly.patch: a ccx linked with an unpatched spooles.a does not link at all, so a binary with the marker has that SPOOLES inside.
A ccx without the second marker keeps the single thread. The value of the check marker is 2 since 24. 11. 2026: a binary built with the first fea_judge prints
FEA_SPOOLES_FACTOR_CHECK=1 (the later factorizations after a verdict "the matrix" still ran unchecked on the threads), and the program gives it ONE thread.

